    "topic_business_finance_intro": "For founders, finance partners, and operators who need to price a SKU, test break-even volume, compare campaign ROI, or sketch NPV/IRR/payback on a project before locking a full model. Tools show the core identities; they do not replace accounting standards or board packs.",
    "topic_business_finance_choose_title": "How these tools fit a deal workflow",
    "topic_business_finance_choose_body": "Start with unit economics (markup, gross/profit/contribution margin, marginal revenue), then capacity questions (break-even), then capital allocation (ROI, NPV, IRR, payback). Keep cash vs accrual timing explicit—NPV/IRR need a consistent discount rate and cash-flow calendar. Use secondary listings (for example discount or VAT) when a shopper-facing tool also informs pricing policy.",
    "topic_business_finance_limits_title": "Boundaries",
    "topic_business_finance_limits_body": "Results ignore multi-entity tax, FX hedges, covenant tests, and IFRS/GAAP presentation choices unless you encode them yourself. IRR can be misleading with non-conventional cash flows; NPV depends entirely on your discount rate. Treat outputs as decision support, not audited financial statements.",
    "topic_business_finance_faq_q1": "Markup vs margin—why do teams confuse them?",
    "topic_business_finance_faq_a1": "Markup is uplift over cost; margin is profit over selling price. A 50% markup is not a 50% margin. Pick the identity your pricing sheet actually uses and convert carefully when talking across teams.",
    "topic_business_finance_faq_q2": "When is payback enough vs NPV/IRR?",
    "topic_business_finance_faq_a2": "Payback answers “how fast cash returns” and ignores time value after recovery. NPV/IRR incorporate discounting and are better when projects differ in scale, timing, or risk. Many ops reviews show both.",
