    "topic_math_stats_intro": "For students, teachers, and analysts who need quick, transparent calculations—central tendency and dispersion, basic inference, combinatorics, GPA, and core algebra/notation—without installing a CAS. Open a tool when you already know which statistic or equation you need.",
    "topic_math_stats_choose_title": "How to choose the right math tool",
    "topic_math_stats_choose_body": "Describe the question in plain language: “typical value” (mean/median), “spread” (standard deviation), “relative to a distribution” (z-score), “evidence against a null” (p-value/CI), “how many people” (sample size), “counting arrangements” (permutation/combination), or “solve/rewrite” (quadratic, exponent, log, sci notation, sig figs, GPA). Prefer tools that echo intermediate steps so homework and peer review stay honest.",
    "topic_math_stats_limits_title": "What these tools do not do",
    "topic_math_stats_limits_body": "They are not a full statistics suite: no mixed models, survey weights, or automated test selection. P-values and CIs assume you chose an appropriate test and that data meet its conditions. GPA scales differ by school; always match your transcript policy.",
    "topic_math_stats_faq_q1": "Mean or median for skewed data?",
    "topic_math_stats_faq_a1": "Medians resist outliers; means use every value and feed many parametric formulas. Report both when distribution shape matters, and state which one your decision uses.",
    "topic_math_stats_faq_q2": "Can a p-value tell me effect size?",
